{"either":{"id":"f081f5a7-d074-4067-bb71-75eef7c29a7a","word":"either","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"introduces the first of two options","word":"ou ... ou"}],"pos":"conj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Either you eat your dinner or you go to your room.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,6]],"type":"example","id":"02576aee-5536-4672-bd27-6be011d3a0d0"},{"text":"You can have either potatoes or rice with that, but not both.","bold_text_offsets":[[13,19]],"type":"example","id":"ad2bde27-4664-4c35-84bb-e3fe556047e9"},{"text":"You'll be either early, late, or on time.","bold_text_offsets":[[10,16]],"type":"example","id":"66d6de81-644b-4dbe-a19c-cd083e6319f8"},{"text":"Either you'll finish your homework or you'll be grounded you home.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,6]],"type":"example","id":"83fa8543-2870-45e1-84e3-5aba675c54dc"},{"text":"You can't be a table and a chair. You're either a Jew or a gentile.","ref":"2006 December 5, Jackie Mason, quotee, “Mason drops lawsuit vs. Jews for Jesus”, in USA Today:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[41,47]],"id":"5ccf11f3-ab8f-4dbb-8862-e7fec16daafb"},{"text":"Thus, when he drew up instructions in lawyer language[…]his clerks[…]understood him very well. If he had written a love letter, or a farce, or a ballade, or a story, no one, either clerks, or friends, or compositors, would have understood anything but a word here and a word there.","ref":"1893, Walter Besant, “Prologue”, in The Ivory Gate:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[174,180]],"id":"48868e4a-5988-411c-af1d-d71535ec2d15"}],"links":[["or","or#English"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"glosses":["Introduces the first of two (or occasionally more) options or possibilities, the second (or last) of which is introduced by “or”."],"id":"45bf6630-2cd7-4470-8b4f-595485749586"}],"related":[{"word":"neither"},{"word":"nor"},{"word":"or"}],"synonyms":[]},"either … or":{"id":"6ebbc2